family  news
# The Usenet set, against a news spool that has something in it.
#
# WHAT THIS CORRECTS.  `DOC/INDEX' said `subscribe' "does NOT" add a group
# and `unsubscribe' "does not" drop one.  BOTH WORK.  The measurement that
# said otherwise used `comp.lang.c', which is not in `.newsrc' at all -- and
# a group that is not there is silently left alone, which looks exactly like
# a program that does nothing. Given a group that IS there, each says so and
# edits the file.
#
# .newsrc IS REWRITTEN IN SETUP, on purpose. These cases change it, and a
# second run of the family would otherwise be measuring the first run's
# leftovers rather than the programs. `:' means subscribed, `!' unsubscribed.

# TERM AND TERMCAP, and they are not decoration: `readnews' and `atp'
# both stop with "Unknown terminal type 'dumb'" without them.  drive.py
# runs every sheet under SYS/login's whole environment and datatest.py
# does not, so a case written from a drive transcript has to set for
# itself whatever the program read out of that environment.
setup   export TERM=vt100
setup   export TERMCAP=/dd/SYS/termcap
setup   export USER=tester
setup   export LOGNAME=tester
setup   export HOME=/dd
setup   export LOGDIR=/dd/LOG
setup   N=/dd/SPOOL/news
setup   W=/dd/tmp/NEWS
setup   /dd/CMDS/mkdir -p $W > /nil 2>/nil
setup   echo "comp.os.os9: 1" > /dd/.newsrc
setup   echo "comp.sys.m68k: 1" >> /dd/.newsrc
setup   echo "news.announce.newusers! 1" >> /dd/.newsrc

case    unsubscribe-turns-a-subscribed-group-off
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/unsubscribe comp.os.os9
run     /dd/CMDS/cat /dd/.newsrc
expect  Newsgroup comp.os.os9 is now unsubscribed.
expect  comp.os.os9! 1
absent  comp.os.os9: 1

case    subscribe-turns-it-back-on
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/subscribe comp.os.os9
run     /dd/CMDS/cat /dd/.newsrc
expect  Newsgroup comp.os.os9 is now subscribed.
expect  comp.os.os9: 1
absent  comp.os.os9! 1

# THE MEASUREMENT THAT MISLED, kept so it cannot mislead again: a group that
# is not in .newsrc is left alone in silence. Read on its own that is
# indistinguishable from a broken program, and it was read that way.
case    a-group-that-is-not-in-newsrc-is-silently-left-alone
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/unsubscribe comp.lang.c
run     /dd/CMDS/cat /dd/.newsrc
expect  comp.os.os9: 1
absent  comp.lang.c

# A BUG IN THE PROGRAM, pinned. The string in the binary is
# "Newsgroup % is already unsubscribed." -- a `%' with no conversion letter,
# so the group name is consumed as a number and a pointer is printed in its
# place. The number differs every run, so only the tail can be asserted.
case    unsubscribe-has-a-format-bug-for-a-group-already-off
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/unsubscribe news.announce.newusers
expect  s already unsubscribed.
absent  Newsgroup news.announce.newusers is already

case    subscribe-and-unsubscribe-print-a-usage-with-no-argument
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/subscribe
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/unsubscribe
expect  usage: subscribe <newsgroup> [newsgroup...]
expect  usage: unsubscribe <newsgroup> [newsgroup...]
expect  if <newsgroup> is 'all', all newsgroups will be unsubscribed

# readnews takes NO options -- `-l' and `-n' both earn its usage line. Bare,
# it reads /dd/SYS/UUCP/active and offers every group that is not yet in
# .newsrc, then updates the file.
# WHAT IS ASSERTED AND WHAT IS NOT.  readnews walks `active' and offers
# every group that is not already in .newsrc -- that is the behaviour, and
# it is what these three lines pin, including the negative: comp.os.os9 IS
# in .newsrc, so it must NOT be offered.
#
# `updating newsrc...' is NOT asserted, and it was, twice.  It comes after
# a final `**** End of newsgroups [yq]' prompt, and whether readnews gets
# that far depends on how the input runs out: one `q' leaves it walking the
# list to the end of file, and three `q's make it end the emulator session
# outright.  A line that needs the input to run out in exactly one way is
# not an assertion about the program.
case    readnews-offers-the-groups-in-active-that-are-not-in-newsrc
run     echo "q" > $W/q.txt
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/readnews < $W/q.txt
expect  New newsgroup: alt.folklore.computers--Subscribe? [yn]
expect  New newsgroup: comp.lang.c--Subscribe? [yn]
expect  New newsgroup: news.announce.important--Subscribe? [yn]
absent  New newsgroup: comp.os.os9

case    readnews-takes-no-options
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/readnews -l < $W/q.txt
expect  readnews: read Usenet news articles
expect  Usage: readnews

case    expire-explains-itself-when-given-an-illegal-option
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/expire -?
expect  expire: illegal option -- ?
expect  news article expire utility
expect  -e<days>   expire articles older than specified number of days
expect  expires all articles older than 14 days

# newslock wants TWO names, a temporary and the lock itself, and says so.
case    newslock-wants-a-tempname-and-a-lockname
run     /dd/CMDS/NEWS/newslock $W/one
expect  Usage: /dd/CMDS/NEWS/newslock tempname lockname
